<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the Info.plist that Tauri generates at bundle time. Only the keys
  Tauri does not already set belong here; everything about the window, version,
  and identifier is generated from tauri.conf.json and must not be duplicated.

  These are TCC usage descriptions. Under the hardened runtime they are not
  cosmetic: they are the sentence the operating system shows a stranger the
  first time the app reaches for their files, and that prompt is roughly the
  fourth thing that happens after a download. A missing string means macOS
  supplies a generic one that explains nothing, on a security-positioned product
  whose entire pitch is that you can see what it does.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and why, so nobody adds it back on a hunch:

    NSMicrophoneUsageDescription / NSCameraUsageDescription
      Nothing captures. Voice is `say(1)`, which is output only (see
      desktop/tauri/src/voice.rs), and there is no getUserMedia anywhere in
      assets/js. The embedded webview browses arbitrary pages, but Tauri's
      WKUIDelegate does not implement requestMediaCapturePermissionFor, so a
      page's capture request is denied before TCC is ever consulted. Declaring a
      permission we never exercise is its own small breach of the trust story.

    NSAppleEventsUsageDescription
      The app scripts nothing. `osascript` appears only under scripts/, which
      runs outside the bundle and therefore outside this entitlement's reach.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- The default workspace for a fresh install is ~/Desktop/BusterClawCLI
       (desktop/tauri/src/workspace.rs). macOS gates the Desktop folder, so this
       prompt fires on first run for essentially every new user. It is the first
       thing the app ever asks for. -->
  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Buster Claw keeps your workspace — the notes, documents, and shift logs your agent reads and writes — in a folder on your Desktop. It only touches that folder.</string>

  <!-- The workspace root is user-choosable, so the same prompt can arrive for
       either of these instead. Same sentence, same promise. -->
  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Buster Claw keeps your workspace — the notes, documents, and shift logs your agent reads and writes — in the folder you chose. It only touches that folder.</string>

  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Buster Claw saves files your agent downloads to this folder, and reads the ones you point it at.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
